00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:53.620
Now let us turn to the core skill of managing defined risk trades in this same policy environment. Credit spread selling lets investors collect premium while defining risk.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:09.300
Exits must be mechanical to avoid emotional overrides. Today's mixed signals from lower inflation prints and geopolitical headlines make rules based exits, especially relevant for Canadian accounts that already face currency and rate volatility.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:21.940
The straightforward rule is to exit any credit spread once the loss equals 100% of the credit originally received. A $13 credit therefore triggers a stop at $26.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:30.500
This approach removes discretion and works on any platform that supports options, including quest trade or interactive brokers.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:42.980
It has historically performed best in range bound or slowly trending markets where premium decay outpaces small adverse moves, yet it can still lose when volatility spikes suddenly.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:49.740
Always size the position so a full stop out equals no more than 1% of portfolio capital.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:03:03.860
The rule applies equally to vertical spreads on individual equities or index ETFs. It pairs naturally with the current environment of contained PCE readings that suggest limited immediate rate cut pressure.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:14.240
Canadian investors using TFSA accounts can implement the same stop discipline without triggering frequent trading concerns because the rule is purely mechanical.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:21.400
That mechanical exit pairs directly with the next step of selecting a name that benefits from the same policy signals.

